+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does South Carolina require a reserve study?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and it requires no reserve fund either. A board can still choose to get one, and a lender can accept a current study in place of its budget test."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays a special assessment voted before closing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The purchase contract decides, so it belongs in the offer. A bill the seller already owes is paid at closing, and a buyer can be held to it only up to the association's written statement of it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see an HOA's reserve records before I buy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Members can. An HOA member can inspect and copy the budget and the accounting records after giving written notice five business days ahead. A buyer is not yet a member, so we ask the seller for them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a newer condo building need strong reserves?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A newer building needs less saved by now. A building finished in 2015 is 11 years old, short of HUD's planning life for its elevator machinery and its flat roof, so less of each has worn out. Percent funded still applies: what the fund holds against that smaller amount."}}]}</script>
